  "finalAnswerPreview": "## Verdict\\n\\nThe ordering guarantee that applies is **global** (Record A): a single durable commit‑observation order per ledger, shared by all readers of that store, independent of any filter. It is **not** per‑consumer (Record B).\\n\\nConcretely, for this workspace (Ordarium workspace `1.3.1`, SQLite schema `v4`, `HOST_CONTRACT_VERSION = 1`):\\n\\n- `StateChangeFeed.changes(filter?, cursor?)` returns revisions in **persistent ledger commit‑observation order** (`changePosition_i < changePosition_j`), assigned by the store inside the same transaction as the state CAS (`change_seq INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT`, `ordarium_state_changes`).\\n- The cursor is an **opaque, global, filter‑independent position**. The same cursor can be reused across different `namespace` filters; it means “skip everything at or below this global position,” not “skip within this consumer’s stream.”\\n- The order implies **no** causality, wall‑clock, or business priority. `written_at` and implicit `rowid` are explicitly not coordination clocks/identity.\\n- “Global” is scoped to **one ledger/store** (correct across independent clients of the same DB, SCF‑INV‑7), not a distributed cross‑store order.\\n\\nRecord ",
